ddingInner` and `paddingOuter` set the gap, `maxBandWidth` stops a wide window from\nstretching a bar."],[1698,"A hairline across the content, or down a row."],[1372,"A header of the app's own still answers Back, and knows where in the stack it is: `header` takes a\nfunction of the page, which is handed the slot the drawn header reads. `back()` is what that header's\nback button does — `onBackPress`, then `pop()` unless `popOnBack` is `false`; `pages()` is how many\nroutes the stack holds, so a header can show a back button only when there is one to go to."],[1374,"A header that is not this one at all is still built from `navPageLook()`'s words — `header()`,\n`title()` and `backGlyph()` — as any content of the app's is:"],[1373,"A header that is the one `NavPage` draws with something added to it composes `PageHeader` instead of\ndrawing the bar again: it keeps the back button, the title and the trailing slot in the header's\nlook, and Back still calls `onBackPress` before it pops."],[587,"A header with no picture"],[1640,"A hover scroll bar in the shared gallery theme."],[2213,"A hundred folders of a hundred files: 10 100 items. The tree is virtualized, so only the rows in view are made; opening a folder adds its hundred rows without a stall. Give it a height (the tree scrolls inside it) and keep `expanded` and `selected` as signals if you read them."],[1452,"A jump menu"],[1109,"A key cap in a button says what its shortcut is; the label is the button's own type\n(`buttonLabel`), the caps are the keys."],[1095,"A key of a shortcut, alone or in a group."],[1884,"A label above a value for metric grids."],[1127,"A label beside a control that cannot be used dims to half. Set `disabled` where the control's own\n`disabled` is set."],[1128,"A label is a row, so an icon or a switch can sit before its text; a control beside it is the\ncaller's own view, a gap of the caller's `style` apart."],[936,"A label is not associated with its control: NSH has no `<label for>`, so a screen reader hears the\ncontrol's own `accessibilityLabel`, then the `FieldLabel` as separate text. Give the control the\nsame words: `<Input accessibilityLabel=\"Email\" />` beside `<FieldLabel>Email</FieldLabel>`. A\ncontrol that carries its own label (`<Checkbox>Hard disks</Checkbox>`) needs neither."],[1469,"A label on a tile"],[1359,"A large title"],[2207,"A large tree"],[1050,"A least height of your own is how the box starts taller than one line, since `style` goes over the look's — 64 points:"],[938,"A legend one step smaller over a group of checkboxes or switches: `variant=\"label\"`."],[1156,"A line in a conversation that is not a message: a status, a note, a labeled separator."],[966,"A line or two: 12 points of padding and 4 between rows."],[1504,"A line or two: 12 points of padding and 4 between rows."],[1189,"A line under the label"],[1810,"A line, an avatar, a button"],[1621,"A link bubble"],[1395,"A link in a panel"],[1644,"A list"],[825,"A list of actions"],[1670,"A list of choices from a field trigger, on the shared listbox look."],[1591,"A list that reloads when it is pulled. `refreshing` is the app's: it is set in `onRefresh` and cleared when the reload ends, and the indicator draws it."],[668,"A long list"],[1070,"A long list"],[21,"A look that more than one component uses stays exported, or lives in `lib/`: `buttonLook` and `iconButtonLook` from the button file, `pressableLook` from the pressable file, `fieldLook` from `lib/ui/field-look.ts`, and `choiceLook` from `lib/ui/choice.tsx`. A look only that file uses is not exported."],[2257,"A look turns a `variant`, a `size`, a `tone`, and a state into the props `nsh/base` draws with. In a copied file the private helpers sit below the public shell. Change a height, a radius, or a hover fill there, and keep the props the base control reads. Helpers only that file uses stay private."],[1016,"A magnifier before the control, the work after it: a spinner while the query settles, then a button\nthat clears it. Search on what the field settles on, not on every key — 300 ms after the last one\nis enough, and the spinner says the search is under way. Put the matches under the field, with the\nletters that matched in the accent, so the answer reads as the field's."],[375,"A main action and the button that opens its menu are one group. Filled buttons (`default`,\n`secondary`) take a `ButtonGroupSeparator` so the seam stays a hairline on the accent face. Outline\nbuttons join on their own edge, the same way shadcn's dropdown split does."],[404,"A main action and the menu of its variants, in one control, is a Button group used as a split button."],[1165,"A marker that acts"],[1172,"A marker that is still going takes a `Spinner` in `MarkerIcon`. NSH has no `status` role and no\nlive-region prop, so a marker whose change must be announced takes `role=\"alert\"`, the one it has\nfor this."],[681,"A menu at the pointer, on the shared menu look."],[1183,"A menu of items, separators and submenus on the shared menu look."],[1002,"A message composer"],[439,"A month of days to pick a date or a range, on the shared calendar look."],[2325,"A native menu draws its own items, so it takes an image rather than a view: an item's `icon` is what `<Image>`'s `src` takes, fitted to the menu's icon size. `iconImage` writes a lucide node as that image, stroked in `currentColor`, so the menu draws it in its own text color:"],[1622,"A node in the text"],[168,"A note and no title"],[169,"A note of the app's own"],[160,"A notice of the whole page or form, read out when it appears."],[206,"A number (`16 / 9`) or CSS's own ratio (`\"16 / 9\"`) say the same; `\"auto\"` lets the height follow\nthe content instead."],[17,"A one-line purpose, then imports."],[753,"A page of its own"],[1367,"A page's `title` is the system header's on iOS and Android, and on the desktops and the web — which\nhave no system header — it is the one `NavPage` draws: a bar's height (the theme's tallest height\nstep, and never under 48 points — shadcn's `h-12`), on the page's fill, a hairline under it, the\ntitle the theme's `title` type role semibold and the room between the back button and the trailing\nslot. It takes the roles the theme keeps for a header wherever it comes from\n(`header.headerStyle`: the page's fill, the text color, the accent for the back button's tint)."],[580,"A page's header that shrinks into a bar as the page scrolls, its picture sliding under the title."],[817,"A panel from the bottom edge, with a grabber, that a swipe closes."],[1714,"A panel that slides in from an edge of the screen over a scrim."],[1402,"A panel: `NavigationMenuContent` mounts `nsh/base` Popover on the\nshared popup surface (`popupSurface`: one edge, the menu tier's shadow, the theme's radius) 10 points\nof room inside (12 on a touch screen), placed against its trigger — `bottom` and `start` unless\n`placement`, `align` and `offset` say otherwise — so it takes that trigger's width unless `width`\nnames another. What goes in it is the app's: a grid of links, a card\nwith a description, a form."],[2324,"A part that takes an icon as a prop, such as `SelectTrigger`'s `icon` or `MarkerIcon`'s, takes the lucide node itself (`icon={Calendar}`) and draws it in its own size and color."],[1051,"A password field that says what it asks for and how far the reader has got: four segments, one for\neach rule the password meets, the word for the score, and the rules under the field — a dot until\nit is met, a tick on the tone that says it is. Score the field as it is typed, and score nothing as\nnothing: an empty field asks for a password rather than calling it weak."],[1041,"A password with a strength meter"],[1521,"A picture"],[264,"A picture that fails to load leaves the initials on the ring, as a gone picture should."],[198,"A picture that keeps its shape"],[1972,"A pill track or underlined row with a traveling indicator."],[2150,"A popover-coloured hint bubble on any element's `tooltip` prop."],[1490,"A popup panel that grows from its trigger, on the shared menu surface."],[1404,"A press opens and closes a panel (Return or Space with the keyboard on the trigger), and on a desktop\nthe pointer's rest opens one too: 200 ms on a trigger (`delayDuration`), and the panel closes 300 ms\nafter the pointer has left both the trigger and the panel (`closeDelay`) — time to cross from one to\nthe other, not to browse. `Infinity` turns that off: only presses and the keyboard, or only Escape and\na press outside. A touch screen has no pointer to rest, so a tap opens and closes there."],[1532,"A press sinks a pressable to 98%. `scale` is that much and nothing else: `0.8` for a dot that gives under a thumb, `1` for a row that should not move. Nothing sinks with reduced motion, or in a theme that draws no press (`press.scale` of 1)."],[1531,"A pressable is not an on / off control: `role=\"switch\"` does not make one a switch, and it has no\n`checked` — its state is `selected`. For a setting, use `Switch`, which owns the switch role and\nits value for screen readers — either the whole row (the switch's children are its label, so a tap\nanywhere toggles it) or a `Field` with the switch at the end of the row. See `Switch`."],[955,"A preview card that grows from its trigger, on the shared popover surface."],[1991,"A program's output on the theme's field surface, the keys typed back to it."],[1149,"A quiet link sits in a footer: muted, with no resting underline."],[998,"A radius of your own"],[2076,"A raised banner with a title, optional description, action and close."],[729,"A range"],[200,"A ratio written as CSS does"],[1997,"A real program"],[2186,"A reasoning line counts while the model thinks (`status=\"running\"`: a `Spinner` in the icon slot, the title pulsing) and then reads what it took. The pulse stands still when the user asks for less motion. It opens onto the text. `open` with `onOpenChange` lets the app open it when the thought is done."],[1020,"A ref on the control focuses it from code — a shortcut, a request to focus a draft. The ref is the control's, not the group's."],[1530,"A region says what it is with `role` (`role=\"button\"` is the default, `role=\"link\"`, `role=\"tab\"`, …): that is NSH's own name for it, and React Native's `accessibilityRole` is not it. What a region _is_ — a link, a key in a toolbar — is the `role`; its state is `selected` and `disabled`, the base button's own props, which screen readers say. There is no `accessibilityState` prop: React Native's state names are not NSH's."],[1515,"A region that takes presses and adds only their feedback."],[1520,"A region with its own look"],[1329,"A reply arrives a few characters at a time. While it does, `TextView` takes\n`options={{ streaming: true }}` and a `streamingCaret` — a `View` with `textViewCaretClass`, the\ntheme's brand-colored block that pulses — where the next word will land. Before the first word\nthere is no text to hang the caret on, so draw the caret alone in a line as tall as the reply's\ntext and leave the view out."],[1316,"A reply that is still arriving"],[1852,"A ring with its top arc in `tint`, turning once every 800 ms; its stroke is an eighth of its side, at least 1.5 points.\nA groove behind the arc is the theme's track form to say: under the sunk form the rest of the ring follows\n`tint` at a quarter, under the tint and hairline forms there is none."],[1425,"A rolling figure"],[249,"A round picture or name-colored initials on the identity ring."],[1748,"A row"],[1892,"A row"],[2212,"A row is `treeRowHeight` tall: 28 points with a pointer, 44 on a touch screen. A well that should be\nas tall as its first rows, with the rest scrolling, sets its own height from it (`Tree`'s `style`),\nplus the list's 6 points of padding on each side."],[1772,"A row keeps its name only while the column is open: nothing else moves, so a look is written once\nwhere the row draws. A row the app draws hands its look the column's fold — `open` and `collapsible`,\nthe app's own signals — and its name goes; a look given no fold options draws the open row, and an\naction or a badge inside a folded item leaves the tree."],[1061,"A row of a list: what sits before its content, a title with a note, and controls after it."],[1220,"A row of menus whose triggers stand in one bar, as a desktop app's menu bar."],[983,"A row of photographs, each a `Pressable` that sets the image; one viewer serves them all."],[885,"A row of quick emoji beside the picker is the same pressables in a pill: six of them, a divider,\nthen a `Popover` whose trigger is a plus, with the same `EmojiPanel` inside it and `react` as its\n`onSelect`."],[2117,"A row of single-select capsules is a recipe, not a part. shadcn/ui has no capsule axis on `ToggleGroup`; where it wants one it puts `rounded-full` on the items. Here the same call-site `style` does it: `inset={false}` drops the track, `gap` spaces the chips, and `borderRadius: 999` makes the capsule. A capsule is geometry, not a theme role (`shape.control` is the corner of a badge), so the radius is the full one. The base group holds no value: the app presses the chip asked for and keeps the chosen one pressed. For a row that scrolls, put the group in a horizontal `Scroll`."],[1388,"A row of triggers, each opening a panel of anything under the bar — one at a time."],[1752,"A row that is still coming"],[1069,"A row that takes presses"],[1227,"A row with its key equivalent"],[1929,"A row with its label is the target: put the label in `children` and let `listRow` draw the row."],[1478,"A row's chart is a `Plot` with `interactive={false}`: no cross line, no hover dot, just the line. The\nwash under it is `plotWash`, the previous close a dashed `Grid` at that value with\n`plotReference`, and the newest point a second `Plot` over the first — only the last row in it — so\nits `Dot` can be a ring around a solid centre."],[1197,"A row's height in the size table is its least: give it a `View` holding its label and a note under it (`menuItemNote`) and the row grows to hold both, growing over the theme's row inset so it never touches the row after it. A neighbour with one line keeps the one-line height."],[1001,"A search field"],[661,"A search palette with sectioned, virtualized rows."],[626,"A searchable field with a list of choices and chips for the values chosen, on the shared listbox look."],[720,"A selection of several is the app's too: set `selectOnModifierPress={false}`, keep your own range or\nset in `onRowPress` / `onColumnPress` (each reports Shift, Control, Option and the clicks), and paint\nit with `rowClasses`, `cellClasses` or the columns' `headerClass` / `cellClass`."],[304,"A separator is a chevron; its children replace it (a slash, a dot, a small icon)."],[1709,"A separator is usually a line of the layout: `decorative` is `true` by default, and a screen reader\nwalks from one group of content to the next without stopping on it. `decorative={false}` keeps it\nin the accessibility tree, as a `role=\"group\"` (`nsh` has no `separator` role — the nearest is a\nboundary between groups); give it an `accessibilityLabel` saying what it divides."],[297,"A separator of your own"],[920,"A set of fields under a legend: a label, its control, a description and errors that are read out."],[1922,"A settings row"],[1730,"A sheet of filters edits a draft, and the footer decides whether the page sees it. Copy the applied\nfilters in when the sheet opens, keep the draft in the content, and write it back only in the\nfooter's button; closing any other way throws the draft away."],[1262,"A short thread, and an arrival"],[1603,"A sidebar"],[2113,"A size is a step of the theme's own scales: its `side` the three heights (`dimension.size.height`),\nits `icon` the three icons (`dimension.icon`), its label the `detail`, `body` and `label` type roles.\nThe NSH Gallery theme's heights are 24 / 28 / 32 points with a pointer and 32 / 40 / 48 on a touch\nscreen, where a hit slop makes the target 44 points."],[405,"A size is compact with a pointer (24 / 28 / 34 points) and roomier on a touch screen (36 / 40 / 48), where a hit slop makes the target 44 points. `sm`, `default` and `lg` are the only values: a pixel size is a geometry prop (`width`, `side`), not a scale."],[1815,"A skeleton has no size of its own: the box is the caller's `style`. A line is as tall as the type\nit will hold and as wide as that text runs; an avatar or an icon is a square, round with\n`borderRadius` half its side."],[492,"A slide that holds a photograph draws its caption itself: the theme has no text role that reads\nover an arbitrary picture, so the ink is `on-media` (white in every theme, both appearances) and\nthe veil behind it is `scrim`. A text role such as `foreground` flips with the appearance and\nleaves the caption unreadable in one of them."],[273,"A small capsule label in a neutral, toned, or outline style."],[1470,"A sparkline"],[407,"A spinner in the icon's place (before the label when there is no icon), and no second press. A new icon, or the spinner, grows in on `motion.control`; with reduced motion it switches at once. The width follows the label at once: a layout animation would move every frame of the update, app-wide."],[199,"A square"],[410,"A square icon-only button; `size` takes four: `xs` 20 points across with a pointer (32 on a touch\nscreen), `sm` 24 (36), `default` 28 (40) and `lg` 32 (44), where a hit slop makes the target 44\npoints and the icon grows with the square. `lg` is upstream's 32 pt tool square, for a rich text\ntoolbar; `xs` is a tab's close × beside a document's name. `selected` (a base prop) shows it as\nchosen."],[1943,"A standalone switch adds `focusRing()` shaped to the capsule track and handles disabled appearance directly (dimming via `dimmed.disabled` when `controls.disabled.face` is `keep`, or switching to `disabled.*` surface/ink/edge on Material 3 and WinUI 3)."],[305,"A step is `nsh/base`'s Link, so `href` and `openWith` work as they\ndo everywhere else: `\"system\"` opens the URL outside the app, `\"app\"` hands it to `onOpen`."],[298,"A step with a destination"],[1522,"A stretch of text"],[1506,"A string child is a `Button` (`variant`, `secondary` by default). A single element child is\nthe trigger itself, with no wrapper: its look, `testID`, name, own press, hover, active and\nfocus styles all stay, and `nsh/base` adds the open state to it, so `expandedStyle` and\n`expanded:` classes answer the popup. `variant`, `disabled` and `style` shape a string\nchild; an element brings its own (its `disabled` is the one that counts)."],[968,"A string child is a `Button` (`variant`, `secondary` by default). A single element child is\nthe trigger itself: its look, `testID`, name and own press and hover styles stay, and\n`nsh/base` adds the open state (`expandedStyle`) to it. `variant` and `disabled` shape a\nstring child; an element brings its own `disabled`, and the root's `disabled` still keeps\nthe card closed."],[147,"A string child is a `Button`; `variant` picks its look (`secondary` by default). Anything else is\ndrawn as given."],[649,"A string child is a `Button`; anything else is drawn as given. `testID` reaches the\nbutton or the default toggle, and `accessibilityLabel` names it (the default toggle is a\nchevron with no text of its own)."],[2166,"A string child is a `Button`; anything else is the element itself, and its own press, hover\nand name stay."],[1234,"A string child is a row's label; a label and a note at the row's trailing edge (`MenubarShortcut`,\nwhich is `MenuShortcut`) are written out — the note takes the rest of the row:"],[409,"A string child is also the button's name for screen readers — they read the text it draws — so `<Button onPress={retry}>Retry</Button>` needs nothing else: a test finds it as `role:button[Retry]`. `accessibilityLabel` still wins when the name should differ from the visible text. Children drawn as given are named by the texts inside them; a button that draws no text needs the `accessibilityLabel` yourself, and an `IconButton` or `RoundButton` (an icon and no text) will not compile without one."],[1202,"A string child is drawn as the button's label in the theme's look (`variant`, `ghost` by default;\n`size` as `Button`'s). It is not a `Button` inside: `nsh/base` makes the button's own view the\npressable on the desktops, and a nested one would take the press. A single element child is the\ntrigger itself, with no wrapper: its look, `testID`, name, own press and hover styles stay, and\n`nsh/base` adds the open state to it (`expandedStyle`, `expanded:` classes)."],[1529,"A string child is plain text, and its text is the region's name for screen readers unless `accessibilityLabel` says otherwise. Children drawn as given are named by the texts inside them; one that draws no text needs an `accessibilityLabel` (the icon-only case is an `IconButton`, where it is required). `accessibilityHint` says what a press does, read after the name."],[1152,"A string child is the link's name for screen readers too, so `<Link href=\"/docs\">Documentation</Link>` needs nothing else; `accessibilityLabel` wins when the name should differ from the text. Children drawn as given are named by the texts inside them."],[518,"A string child is the row's label and its name for screen readers, so the box and its text are one control to a reader; `accessibilityLabel` wins when it should differ. Children drawn as given are named by the texts inside them."],[1203,"A string child is the row's label and its name for screen readers; `accessibilityLabel` wins when it should differ. Children drawn as given are named by the texts inside them."],[1575,"A string child is the row's label and its name for screen readers; `accessibilityLabel` wins when it should differ. Children drawn as given are named by the texts inside them."],[1770,"A string child is the row's name. Anything else is drawn as given, which is how an icon leads a name:\nthe look has the words for it."],[2118,"A string child is the toggle's name for screen readers, and `accessibilityLabel` wins when it should differ from the visible label. Children drawn as given (an icon and a label of your own) are named by the texts inside them."],[777,"A string child of `DialogTrigger` or `DialogClose` is a `Button`; `variant` picks its look (`secondary` by default). Anything else is drawn as given."],[408,"A string is the label, and a child that changes (`{count()}` beside other children) is followed the same way. Anything else is drawn as given; `buttonLabel` and `buttonGlyph` give it the button's type and icon size."],[703,"A striped table with sortable, resizable columns."],[1750,"A sub menu"],[506,"A sunk box that fills in citrus, with a check or a dash springing in."],[1419,"A sunk number field with − / + or stacked chevron steppers."],[1822,"A sunk rail, citrus fill, and round thumb with a focus ring."],[1560,"A sunk ring that fills in citrus, with a dot growing in — alone or as a choice tile."],[2038,"A sunk well for a time of day, one segment at a time."],[686,"A surface"],[2406,"A surface inside another takes a concentric corner from its outer one: `rounded(outer, inset)` gives `outer − inset`, never below 0. `rounded()` also puts the corner on with `shape.curve` (`\"continuous\"` or `\"circular\"`):"],[2375,"A surface is light and depth: what a layer is filled with, and how it sits above what is behind it. There are eleven, and each states the same fields in every theme — `background`, `ink`, `gradient`, `border`, `borderTop`, `borderBottom`, `shadow`, `backdropFilter`, `material` and `elevation` — either for both appearances or split into `light` and `dark`. A field a surface leaves out is not missing: it is a neutral (no gradient, no material, no shadow), and a shadow names the parts of the theme's own scale (`[\"edgeSurface\", \"sm\"]`) instead of writing one, so `elevation(\"md\")` gives the same values on its own."],[822,"A swipe closes it"],[1930,"A switch is the whole row here — its children are its label, so a tap anywhere on the row toggles it — and it is read as a switch that is on or off, because `Switch` sets its own role and its own value. Nothing else has to say either."],[815,"A tab as `renderTab` and `renderDragPreview` receive it is `DockTabValue`: `nsh/base`'s `DockTab`, named so here because `DockTab` is the component the tab is drawn with."],[331,"A tail"],[1110,"A terminal draws no cap: a key is its name in the monospaced face, one Cell row high, with no fill\nand no edge."],[909,"A terminal draws the block as its lines: no padding, one Cell row between the words and the\nactions, the title and the description as plain text."],[1995,"A terminal in the page"],[2003,"A terminal is a stream of bytes in and out; the app owns both ends."],[126,"A text field"],[1033,"A text field — sunk, ghost, multiline, masked, or invalid."],[1139,"A text link with underline motion, on the shared nav look."],[2238,"A theme is a `ThemeSpec` compiled by `defineTheme()`. Pass another theme's `spec` as `extends` and state only what you change. `selectTheme()` applies the result while the app runs. `selectTheme(null)` returns to the theme the app was built with."],[2528,"A theme is one `AppTheme`: the TypeScript spec `defineTheme()` compiles — tokens, metrics, elevation,\neleven surfaces, feedback, motion and controls — and the `theme.json` that same build exports from it\n(the roles of both appearances, three `radius-*` layer lengths and the fixed `scrim` / `media` /\n`on-media` values). A theme that ships beside another is a sparse delta over it, so it states only\nwhat it changes. An app is _built_ with one theme and can _carry_ more, switching between them while\nit runs with `selectTheme`."],[2422,"A theme of your own is a `defineTheme()` delta over one that ships: `defineTheme({ id, label, extends: nshGallery.spec, tokens: { … } })`, sparse — what it does not state, it inherits. `ALL_THEMES` in `packages/theme/themes` is the list, and adding one there gives it a page on this site and a registry item."],[2164,"A title and a note in a column, 10 points of padding."],[166,"A title and no note"],[1933,"A title beside a switch takes no presses, so the switch is the target — a touch screen gives it the room a finger needs (`hitSlop`) — and a row that should toggle on a tap anywhere is the `listRow` row above."],[2180,"A tool call"],[412,"A tool in a rich text toolbar, at upstream's 32 pt:"],[2167,"A tooltip hangs on the element the trigger is. Give that element the `tooltip` prop and\nhand it to the trigger that wants the press; do not nest `TooltipTrigger` around a trigger\npart, which draws nothing for the hint's host to wrap."],[1767,"A touch screen, or a window narrower than 600 points, shows the column as a sheet instead — this\nlibrary's `Sheet`, 288 points wide from the side the column stands on, with the same trigger opening\nit. The column's own state is left alone, so a window that widens again finds it as it was."],[1653,"A track of mutually exclusive choices whose thumb springs to the chosen one."],[295,"A trail"],[1893,"A trend beside a figure"],[1401,"A trigger is the theme's tallest height step high and 16 points in either side, its name in the\nbar's type and a chevron after it — the theme's smallest icon, an indicator's."],[101,"A trigger of its own"],[636,"A trigger of your own"],[1499,"A trigger of your own"],[2158,"A trigger of your own"],[962,"A trigger of your own, or a disabled one"],[1679,"A trigger that takes the room of a row"],[332,"A turn that acts"],[1331,"A turn that fails leaves two marks. The sent turn keeps its place, with a `Spinner` and\n`Waiting for a connection` in its footer while the app retries. The assistant's turn holds a\ndestructive `Alert`: an `AlertCircle`, `AlertTitle` naming the failure, and an `AlertDescription`\nwhose `Retry` button asks for the turn again."],[1842,"A turning ring: work going on."],[2095,"A two-state button, in two variants and