lation"],[1038,"Invalid"],[1676,"Invalid"],[995,"Invalid and disabled"],[1376,"iOS shows `UINavigationController`'s header and Android its toolbar, with the system's transitions,\nthe swipe back and predictive back; this component draws nothing of the header there, and `title`,\n`headerLeft`, `headerRight`, `headerStyle`, `headerLargeTitle`, `hidesBottomBarWhenPushed` and\n`statusBar` are the platform's. The header does not follow the theme unless the app says so: pass\n`headerStyle={header.headerStyle}` from `@shadcn-nsh/theme` for the page's fill, the text color and\nthe accent, which is what the desktop header wears already."],[1406,"It goes somewhere with `href` and `openWith`, or reports a press — and on the web a link with an\n`href` shows no hover of its own, so a navigation menu's links are usually `onPress`. A `style` goes\nover the look's: the demo's leading card is one link on the inset surface of\n`lib/ui/surface` (`insetSurface()`)."],[1375,"It is a function, not a bare element, because `nsh/base` mounts the header in the page's own header\nhost: what the page provides around its content does not reach there, so an element passed as\n`header` could not read the page it heads."],[54,"It writes `theme.json`, `DESIGN.md` and the theme's TypeScript under `lib/ui/` into the app (under `packages/ui` in a monorepo), and a `tsconfig.json` when the app has none. `theme.json` and that TypeScript are the theme the app is **built** with; which theme they hold is recorded in `nsh-ui.json` (`\"theme\": \"theme\"`, or `\"theme-shadcn\"` for `--theme shadcn`), and `add` resolves a component's `theme` dependency to it, so taking a component later — `--overwrite` included — never writes the build-time theme back. `theme.json` is the build export — the roles of both appearances, the three radii and the fixed `scrim` / `media` / `on-media` values — `DESIGN.md` gives coding agents the same theme in words, and neither follows later edits to the TypeScript. `--theme shadcn` also writes `themes/shadcn.ts`, the same palette as a theme the app can switch to while it runs."],[1060,"Item"],[1094,"Kbd"],[688,"Keep it open"],[1260,"Keys"],[1116,"Label"],[850,"Languages"],[3,"Layers"],[594,"Leave `CollapsingHeaderBackdrop` out: the header is its fill, the description and the title, and\nstill collapses. A terminal draws no picture either way."],[642,"Leave `multiple` out. A choice replaces the last one and closes the list. `onChange` receives `{ value: string | null }`."],[1592,"Leave `refreshIndicator` and `pullValue` off and the platform draws its own spinner; there is nothing to mount. Use this when the platform's look is what you want."],[809,"Leave the render props out and the area uses `DockTab` and `DockDragPreview`. The area's own classes are the chrome of every part, and a class you pass follows them."],[1465,"Legend"],[1466,"Line and area"],[532,"Line numbers"],[533,"Lines of your own"],[1138,"Link"],[40,"list"],[95,"List rows that reveal panels with a turning chevron."],[2270,"list, view, diff"],[67,"Lists the registry's items, marking the ones the app has."],[2272,"Local registry"],[617,"Look helpers: `colorAreaLook`, `colorSliderLook`, `formatColor`, `inkOn`. Parts: `ColorFields`, `ColorTrigger`, `ColorSwatches`, `ColorCopy`, `ColorDropper`. Behavior stays in `nsh/base` ColorPicker parts."],[1204,"Looks are `menuItemLook`, `menuItemLabel`, `menuItemNote`, `menuItemGlyph`, `menuPopup`, `menuSeparator` and `menuHighlight`; `menuItemSize.height` is a row's least height, not its height; `SubmenuRow` and `SubmenuBack` draw a submenu's row and its touch back row. Every prop of `nsh/base` Menu on `MenuContent` passes through except open state and `trigger`. There is no radio-group part: a `MenuGroup` holds the rows, and each `MenuRadioItem` carries its own `checked`."],[2426,"Maintaining this repository: edit the look in `packages/ui` (all of it is ours; a credit line in the header says where it began). After editing `packages/theme`, run `bun run theme:build` and `bun run registry:build`, and commit `public/r`."],[2016,"Markdown"],[1155,"Marker"],[1546,"Marker"],[2447,"Material"],[2448,"Material 3 Expressive: tonal surfaces, capsule controls, circular corners, 8/10/10% state layers, and expressive shapes."],[1068,"Media"],[1182,"Menu"],[1219,"Menubar"],[1306,"Message"],[1252,"Message scroller"],[2271,"Monorepo"],[750,"Motion"],[1812,"Motion"],[1040,"Multiline"],[58,"Name at least one item, or pass `--all`."],[394,"Names for screen readers"],[514,"Names for screen readers"],[1146,"Names for screen readers"],[1193,"Names for screen readers"],[1569,"Names for screen readers"],[2108,"Names for screen readers"],[66,"Naming a theme (`add theme-shadcn`) installs **one more**: `themes/shadcn.ts`, plus `themes/shadcn/theme.json` and `DESIGN.md`. The app switches to it with `selectTheme`. The module is a sparse `defineTheme()` delta, so it states only what it changes. It writes nothing over the app's build-time theme, and `nsh-ui.json` stays as it is; that theme is `init --theme <name> --overwrite`."],[1124,"Naming the control"],[1387,"Navigation Menu"],[1348,"NavStack"],[2279,"No `package.json` and no `node_modules`. `selectTheme` switches a built-in theme while the app runs. Tailwind token names such as `bg-surface` and `rounded-(--radius-md)` still need a build-time `theme.json`, which CLI `init` writes."],[1045,"No fill and no edge until the keys are in it, with the text, the placeholder and the focus ring as they are: a search field, a title that turns into a field. Disabled still dims, and invalid still shows the danger edge."],[1934,"Nothing in the row names a side: `Field` lays its children out along the writing direction, so a right-to-left app puts the switch first and the text against the other edge, with the row unchanged."],[2007,"Nothing of that is the terminal's: it interprets the escape sequences it is given and draws them\nwith the platform's text engine. NSH's Terminal documents the\nescape sequences it understands and how a Rust app replaces the emulator (`TerminalEmulatorFactory`)."],[2466,"NSH Gallery"],[2417,"NSH takes the first family of the list that is not a generic one (`system-ui`, `sans-serif`, `monospace` …), and a list of generics alone is the system's own font. That is why this library writes `monospace`: the system's monospaced font, not a font called \"monospace\"."],[1418,"Number Input"],[1948,"Off and on, the knob wears `control(\"switch\").knobStyle(checked)` (`packages/ui/switch.tsx`, `knobLook`): the theme states its size, face, gradient and edge per state, and how it grows when pressed."],[1264,"Older turns"],[2159,"On a menu's trigger"],[1358,"On a phone"],[1747,"On a phone"],[1862,"On a phone"],[2157,"On any element's `tooltip` prop"],[1377,"On iOS `headerLargeTitle` is the platform's: a large title in the content that shrinks into the bar\nas the page scrolls. The desktops and the web have no such header, so the page draws one: the large\ntitle is the first thing in the `Scroll`, and a header of the app's own keeps the small title in the\nbar once the large one has scrolled past."],[16,"One component is one file. The public part is the top of the file. The private part is the bottom."],[2234,"One control's look"],[974,"One image full screen over a dimmed ground, fitted, with pinch to zoom, drag to pan and swipe down to close."],[2373,"One ink serves them all: a card, a popover, a secondary and an accent surface read `foreground` unless the surface states its own, which `surface(role).ink` returns (see Surfaces). `tints.brand`, `tints.info`, `tints.success`, `tints.warning` and `tints.destructive` are the same tones as low fills behind text, and `toneColor(tone)` / `toneTint(tone)` pick either from the `Tone` a prop carries — `\"neutral\"`, `\"accent\"`, `\"info\"`, `\"success\"`, `\"warning\"` or `\"destructive\"`."],[2526,"One look, more than one palette"],[1307,"One row of a conversation: the sender's avatar, who they are, the turn, and what is under it."],[6,"One source, two installs"],[2339,"One TypeScript spec is the look; theme.json and DESIGN.md are its exports, and lib/ui/theme.ts reads it for every component."],[632,"One value"],[1433,"One-time code cells that lift out of sunk wells as digits fill in."],[1403,"Only one panel is open at a time, and the root owns which: `value` (controlled) or `defaultValue`,\nreported by `onValueChange({ value, reason })`. The open trigger wears the `accent` fill at half and\nits chevron turns 180° — at once under reduced motion, in 200 ms otherwise."],[1832,"Opacity on the track comes from the look's `disabled:opacity-50` class on `trackClass`."],[567,"Open by default"],[690,"Open change"],[1259,"Opening elsewhere"],[834,"Opening from the bottom by default, the grabber, the swipe, and the four parts around the panel\n(`DrawerHeader`, `DrawerTitle`, `DrawerDescription`, `DrawerFooter`). The panel, the scrim and the\ntitle's type are shared: `sheetPanel(side)`, `backdrop()`, `dialogTitle()` and `dialogText()`, so a\ndrawer and the rest of the library wear one surface."],[1702,"Orientation"],[1432,"OTP Input"],[1678,"Outline trigger, and a width"],[1123,"Over a group"],[2134,"Overflow"],[2348,"Override from nsh.json"],[1446,"Page numbers and chevrons with the current page lit primary."],[1349,"Pages pushed on a stack — the system header on a phone, the app's own themed header on a desktop."],[487,"Pagination"],[1445,"Pagination"],[1424,"Pair steppers"],[608,"Panel"],[2493,"Panes of thick smoky glass: a rim of light on each top edge, a hairline round it, a shadow underneath, 11-point controls and 20-point cards."],[853,"Parts"],[866,"Pass `value` and set it again in `onChange`; the app owns the file, as a formatter's output or a loaded file replaces it."],[1551,"Pass a shared `progressSweep()` from the look and set `indeterminate`."],[255,"Picture"],[219,"Pictures"],[1816,"Placeholders keep the layout of what is coming: a column with a `gap` where the rows will be, and\na row for the parts that sit side by side."],[1498,"Placement"],[2155,"Placement"],[961,"Placement and delays"],[1460,"Plot"],[1489,"Popover"],[258,"Presence"],[751,"Presentation"],[731,"Presets"],[1524,"Press scale"],[1514,"Pressable"],[2107,"Pressed"],[72,"Prints the files `add` would write, dependencies first, at their target paths and with imports rewritten for the app. Writes nothing."],[1537,"Progress"],[2309,"Props"],[2249,"Public component shells sit at the top of each file. Component-private `*Look` helpers live at the bottom. The shell is what you change to add a prop or a part. The private look is what you change to restyle that control. Shared looks stay exported, because other files import them: `buttonLook` and `iconButtonLook` in `components/ui/button.tsx`, `pressableLook` in `components/ui/pressable.tsx`, `fieldLook` in `lib/ui/field-look.ts`, and `choiceLook` in `lib/ui/choice.tsx`."],[1586,"Pull to refresh"],[1143,"Quiet"],[2346,"Radii"],[2527,"Radii by role"],[1559,"Radio"],[861,"Ranges the app marks, carried through edits until it sets new ones: an error is underlined in the theme's `destructive`, a warning in `warn`, information in `info`, a hint in `muted-foreground`."],[1318,"Rating a reply"],[330,"Reactions"],[879,"Reactions"],[23,"Read a color, a surface, or a control slot in JSX or in a getter: `colors.primary`, `surface(\"card\")`, `control(\"button\")`. `selectTheme()` and the light/dark switch then update the next render."],[2374,"Read a role where it is drawn (in JSX, a `draw`, or an effect): the getters read the token then, so they follow the appearance."],[1039,"Read-only and disabled"],[2347,"Reading the theme"],[2179,"Reasoning"],[877,"Recent emoji"],[1581,"RefreshIndicator"],[1597,"Resizable"],[1598,"Resize handles with a quiet hairline and a growing thumb."],[926,"Responsive"],[2350,"Restyling"],[102,"Revealed panel"],[2156,"Rich"],[329,"Rich content"],[1619,"Rich document"],[1615,"Rich document typography and its session API, on nsh/base's RichEditor."],[1614,"Rich Editor"],[124,"Roles"],[512,"Round"],[396,"Round button"],[401,"RoundButton"],[697,"Row and popup looks come from Menu. Items, separators, labels and submenus are re-exported as `ContextMenu*` parts. There is no radio-group part: a `ContextMenuGroup` holds the rows, and each `ContextMenuRadioItem` carries its own `checked`."],[719,"Rows select by default; `columnSelectable` lets titles select columns and `cellSelectable` lets\ncells select. `onSelectionChange` reports the `{ row, column }` selection (both zero-based), and\n`selection` sets it back — a controlled selection, so what stays selected is the app's call. A\nselection of a kind that does not select is cleared."],[671,"Rows with the same `group` gather under its heading, in the order given; `disabled` dims a row."],[1639,"Scrollbar"],[752,"Scrolling"],[667,"Search"],[2048,"Segment chrome lands with the listbox / date-picker family."],[1652,"Segmented"],[1669,"Select"],[390,"Selected"],[1955,"Selected row"],[712,"Selecting"],[1265,"Selecting across turns"],[1162,"Separator"],[1697,"Separator"],[687,"Separator and a destructive item"],[928,"Separators"],[45,"serve"],[89,"Serves the built registry over HTTP at `127.0.0.1` for local testing."],[2416,"Set the app's families in the `cssVars.theme` block of its own `theme.json`:"],[633,"Several values"],[2479,"shadcn/ui"],[1596,"shadcn/ui has no counterpart (the web has no pull), so there is nothing to compare; the name follows the `refreshIndicator` prop it is drawn for."],[1364,"shadcn/ui has no navigation stack, and no page of its own; the behavior is NSH's\n`NavStack` — the pages, the transitions, the back gestures\nand the pages kept mounted with their state. This component is that stack and its pages, plus the\nheader a page shows on the desktops and the web, where NSH draws none."],[2480,"shadcn/ui's neutral palette: near-black and near-white fills, flat surfaces, 8-point controls and 14-point cards."],[1713,"Sheet"],[666,"Shortcut and check"],[536,"Show more"],[549,"Show more is the app's state: cap the content while it is collapsed and put a ghost `Button` in the footer."],[1719,"Side"],[785,"Side by side"],[1738,"Sidebar"],[217,"Size"],[279,"Size"],[388,"Size"],[510,"Size"],[530,"Size"],[1067,"Size"],[1565,"Size"],[1846,"Size"],[1919,"Size"],[2101,"Size"],[2307,"Size and color"],[1926,"Sizes"],[1805,"Skeleton"],[1821,"Slider"],[484,"Slides"],[479,"Snapping slides with arrows and a traveling dot pill."],[186,"So: a notice that can wait belongs here; a question that must be answered before anything else is\nan `alert-dialog` (or `dialog`, when the box needs content of the app's own)."],[2492,"Solid Glass"],[2181,"Sources"],[1703,"Space around it"],[1978,"Spanning the row"],[1841,"Spinner"],[366,"Split button"],[1423,"Split steppers"],[1854,"SplitView"],[1883,"Stat"],[218,"States"],[1164,"Status"],[1904,"Status"],[1547,"Steps"],[296,"Steps left out"],[1330,"Stopping is the app's: it keeps the words already received, replaces the `Assistant` name in the\nheader with `Stopped` (a warn `Badge`) and adds a faint row saying what happened. `Resume` streams\nthe rest through the same signals."],[2017,"Streaming"],[1956,"String children"],[708,"Stripes"],[2235,"Structure and API"],[2219,"Styled components for Native Shell, built on nsh/base. Install with nshm or the copy-into-app CLI."],[2222,"Styled shells on `nsh/base`. Install a component either way:"],[1191,"Submenu"],[2343,"Surfaces"],[2434,"Surfaces"],[2453,"Surfaces"],[2472,"Surfaces"],[2485,"Surfaces"],[2498,"Surfaces"],[2511,"Surfaces"],[610,"Swatches"],[1914,"Switch"],[1949,"Table"],[803,"Tabs"],[1971,"Tabs"],[873,"Tabs, search, skin tones and grid styled for nsh/base EmojiPicker."],[1990,"Terminal"],[2011,"TextView"],[1729,"The `footer` stays at the bottom of the part of the panel that shows, at every detent; the smallest\none has to be tall enough to show it. A `Scroll` above it takes what room is left, so the thread\nmoves under a thumb while the field and its send button stay put."],[2389,"The `material` says what the platform's material should be: `null` (every theme but the glassed ones), `glass` or `acrylic` on the `apple` and `winui` surfaces that float, or a per-platform name. The `apple` theme fills in `glass`, `overlay` and `raised` (Liquid Glass on iOS and macOS 26+), and every other surface is a flat fill with a hairline edge; the `material` theme uses Material 3 Expressive tonal surfaces, circular corners, 8/10/10% state layers, press ripple and Android elevation on raised surfaces; the `winui` theme uses WinUI 3 (Windows 11) fills and strokes, 4/8-point circular corners, Segoe UI Variable, double focus rings and Windows blur on `glass`, `overlay` and `inverse`. `nativeTheme()` from `themes/native` picks the theme of the platform the app runs on — Apple's on iOS and macOS, Material on Android, WinUI 3 on Windows, and the NSH Gallery theme everywhere else:"],[2009,"The 16 ANSI colors are NSH's own palette; text a program leaves uncolored is the theme's\n`foreground`, and cells it leaves without a background show the terminal's own surface."],[899,"The app's own art"],[2432,"The appearance roles"],[2451,"The appearance roles"],[2470,"The appearance roles"],[2483,"The appearance roles"],[2496,"The appearance roles"],[2509,"The appearance roles"],[2326,"The application menu's items take it the same way (`Menu.setApplicationMenu`). `iconImage(icon, options?)`:"],[86,"The argument is the registry file (default `./registry.json`). It fails when an item's `registryDependencies` disagree with its imports."],[2335,"The arrows and chevrons that point along the line (`ChevronLeft`, `ChevronRight`, `ArrowLeft`, `ArrowRight`) are mirrored in a right-to-left layout."],[1356,"The back button"],[1225,"The bar"],[1393,"The bar"],[2139,"The bar a text editor puts over the text it formats: history, the block, the marks, the alignment,\na hairline between the runs. Undo and Redo are `Button`s that gray out at the ends of the history;\nthe marks a `ToggleGroup`; the alignment `Toggle`s that only ever set. The block is a `Menu` whose\n**trigger is one of the bar's items** — a `View` inside `MenuTrigger`, which `nsh/base` hands the\npress, the focus and the expanded state, so the arrow keys reach it like any other tool and Space or\nReturn opens it. Give that view `role=\"button\"`, a name and `accessibilityState` and it reads like\nthe menu button it is. The menu's rows are `MenuRadioItem`s: one block is chosen at a time."],[1251,"The bar and its triggers spread the view props they are given on the row they draw, after the look\n(`style` included), and merge a given `hoverStyle`, `activeStyle`, `expandedStyle` or `disabledStyle`\nafter the look's own. The rows, labels, notes and groups are `menu.tsx`'s, so they take what a menu's\nparts take."],[593,"The bar is a row, so what it holds can act: the title takes the room it needs and a button comes\nafter it, in reach at every height the header has."],[2050,"The bar that drags a desktop window, the window buttons a frameless one draws for itself, and the edges that resize it."],[967,"The base defaults are 600 ms before it opens and 300 ms before it closes."],[1868,"The behavior — which columns show, the phone collapsing to one, the system's sidebar button on iOS — is NSH's `SplitView`. This component is that view, plus the column's surface, the header a desktop draws, and the button that hides the sidebar."],[1817,"The block breathes (an endless motion). When the user asks for less motion it stays still, and a\nterminal shows it still: nothing to play there."],[918,"The block is a centered column padded 24, its header a step of 8 apart from its content, the words\nand the actions no wider than 384 points. The title is medium at `body + 2` (15 points with a\npointer, 17 on a touch screen), the description the body's size in the muted ink, both centered.\n`EMPTY_ICON` is the size an icon takes in the `icon` variant."],[1049,"The box grows only as the app sizes it: `nsh/base`'s multi-line input reports the size of its text (`onContentSizeChange`) rather than sizing itself to it, and the look keeps only a least height — one line's, so a multiline field sits as tall as a one-line input until the app says otherwise. Keep the reported height plus the room the look keeps inside the box:"],[210,"The box is as wide as its column (`width: \"100%\"`) and its height is that width over the ratio; its\ncontent is clipped to it (`overflow: \"hidden\"`), so a picture fills the box rather than spilling out\nof it."],[133,"The box is the platform's own (`nsh/base`'s `AlertDialog.show`): UIAlertController on iOS, NSAlert\non macOS, NSH's drawing of the same box on the web, the platform dialog on Android. The parts only\nfill it in — they register a title, a message and buttons, and `AlertDialogContent` shows it when\nthe root is open. So the box **does not follow this library's theme**, what it shows is **text\nonly** (no views or icons; at most one text field, `input`, where the platform's alert has one), and an Android alert takes **at most three buttons**. The\nplatform shows one box at a time, so a process has one: two `AlertDialog`s opened together share\nit, and the second is not stacked on the first."],[204,"The box takes its column's width and the ratio's height; its content fills it."],[2027,"The built-in copy's accessibility label is the action's `label`; give an app action its own\n`renderCodeBlockAction` and handle `id` in `onCodeBlockAction`."],[2315,"The built-in names are the ones the components draw (`ChevronDown`, `Plus`, `Search`, `X`, and the rest of that set). An icon the set does not include still comes from `lucide`:"],[1130,"The caption of a set of choices, a column's first line."],[760,"The card is 360 points with a pointer and 320 on a touch screen; `width` sets another, never past 90% of the window."],[763,"The card is never taller than the window holds inside the safe areas, 24 points clear of either edge, and its body scrolls under that cap. `DialogFooter` is drawn under the scrolling body, so the actions stay put; `DialogTitle` and `DialogDescription` are in the body and scroll with it (put them in their own block if the title has to stay)."],[495,"The carousel holds no timer: a slide that moves on by itself is the app's — a controlled `index` a\ntimer advances, around the ends. Hold it while the user is at the carousel: the pointer over a\n`View` around it (`onMouseEnter` / `onMouseLeave`), the keyboard focus in it (`onFocus` /\n`onBlur`) and a drag (`onDragStart` / `onDragEnd`), and leave it off with `reduceMotion()`. Key the\neffect on the index, so every change — a timer's, an arrow's, a dot's, the rotation control's —\nstarts a whole interval, or a press would be cut short by the turn already under way. The rotation\ncontrol comes first in the row, beside the dots, and names itself `Pause announcements` or\n`Play announcements`."],[647,"The chips are that `prefix`, not a field of their own: typing beside them still filters through the\ncombobox's input, and a text field of its own beside the chips is a second control."],[125,"The chosen action"],[1982,"The chosen tab is a plate on the track — the theme's `plate`, a labelled block inside the well —\nand a tab's own box is that plate's box, so the pointer's hover shade covers exactly where the pill\nis."],[540,"The code is one color in the theme's monospaced family: there is no syntax highlighting. For\nhighlighted, editable code, use `Editor`, which is `nsh/base`'s."],[2316,"The color is one of the theme's roles, never a literal."],[1744,"The column"],[1869,"The columns' widths are the platform's. Panes the user resizes are `Resizable`."],[2312,"The components draw their own basic UI icons. `lib/ui/icons.tsx` ships those nodes, so a component does not need the `lucide` package at run time. `Icon` strokes a node on a canvas in its 24-unit box."],[1017,"The composer of a chat: a `block-end` addon under the textarea holds a round button that opens\nwhat can be added, the key hint, and the send button. Give the group the composer's radius and\npadding (`borderRadius: 22`, `padding: 6`), and let the textarea grow to five lines with\n`grownFieldHeight` — an `Input` takes the lines' height from `onContentSizeChange`. Return sends\nand Shift-Return breaks the line under a keyboard (`submitBehavior=\"submit\"` on desktop); on a\ntouch screen Return breaks the line, and the button sends."],[207,"The content is anything: a chart, a placeholder, a block's fill with its label centered. A `style`\nof your own goes over the look's, so the fill and the corners are the caller's."],[1731,"The count in the apply button follows the draft while the page's own count still follows `applied`,\nso the difference between \"what I am choosing\" and \"what is on\" is visible at every press. `Reset`\nclears the draft only; the footer's other button is still what applies it."],[884,"The counted chips are a row of pressables under the bubble: one tap adds yours or takes it back\noff, and a count that reaches zero drops the chip. `BubbleReactions` draws a row of emoji alone —\nits children are text, and the room it keeps is a line of it — so counted chips go in the\n`MessageContent` below the bubble."],[1407,"The current page is often a plain link standing where a trigger would:"],[2001,"The default: a well of the field's fill and edge, the theme's inner corners and a padding, so a\nterminal sits beside the other controls."],[2424,"The demos on this site can draw in an installed theme: the **Demo theme** switch in the nav (Default, shadcn, Solid Glass, Apple, Material or WinUI 3) writes the demo app's palette and loads the panels again. The list is `apps/website/demos/palettes.ts`; a demo reads the palette before it mounts, so its first frame is already the chosen theme. Every palette — its install command and every role it sets — has a page linked from Themes."],[1479,"The domain is padded by a fifth of the range so the ring is not clipped at the top or the bottom;\n`tone` is the theme's `up` or `down`, the same role the figure beside it uses."],[174,"The examples draw icons with `Icon` from `lib/ui/icons`; see Icons for adding it and picking a size and color."],[225,"The examples draw icons with `Icon` from `lib/ui/icons`; see Icons for adding it and picking a size and color."],[403,"The examples draw icons with `Icon` from `lib/ui/icons`; see Icons for adding it and picking a size and color."],[639,"The examples draw icons with `Icon` from `lib/ui/icons`; see Icons for adding it and picking a size and color."],[904,"The examples draw icons with `Icon` from `lib/ui/icons`; see Icons for adding it and picking a size and color."],[1008,"The examples draw icons with `Icon` from `lib/ui/icons`; see Icons for adding it and picking a size and color."],[1073,"The examples draw icons with `Icon` from `lib/ui/icons`; see Icons for adding it and picking a size and color."],[1270,"The examples draw icons with `Icon` from `lib/ui/icons`; see Icons for adding it and picking a size and color."],[1322,"The examples draw icons with `Icon` from `lib/ui/icons`; see Icons for adding it and picking a size and color."],[1755,"The examples draw icons with `Icon` from `lib/ui/icons`; see Icons for adding it and picking a size and color."],[2162,"The examples draw icons with `Icon` from `lib/ui/icons`; see Icons for adding it and picking a size and color."],[1429,"The field draws its own glyphs, and the caret lives in them, so the digits inside the field do not roll. `Roll` (`@/lib/ui/motion`, the `motion` registry item, which `input` installs) is the figure beside it — upstream's ticker. It rolls on the theme's `control` spring. The first value is already there. Less motion replaces it in place."],[673,"The field filters the rows as you type. The shell sets the search bar's row and the field's text\nand placeholder color inline, over `commandClasses`, so an icon in `searchPrefix` stays beside the\nfield and the placeholder stays the muted foreground — including when `searchable` turns the bar on\nafter the palette has mounted. A caller's `searchBarStyle` and `searchStyle` win over those."],[1626,"The file re-exports the session's API beside the view, so an app imports the editor once: `createRichEditor` and the kits a session starts from (`StarterKit`, `TableKit`), the commands, `defineSchema` / `defineExtension` / `richExtension` and `RichEditorPlugin` for a session of its own, and the types of the session, its parts, its commands, its extensions and its node views. `command(\"<name>\")` runs any Tiptap command by name; `chain`, `undo`, `redo`, `extendMarkRange` and `insertContent` are the named builders a toolbar usually needs."],[2297,"The files install into `packages/ui`, and the app imports them through the workspace:"],[601,"The fill is `surface(\"accent\")` — the primary colour, its ink from `surface(\"accent\").ink`, edged in the\nhairline's `brand`; the title is the display role open and the label role's size collapsed; the\ndescription is the detail's size; the bar's room either side is `density.pad` and its gap\n`density.gap`. In a terminal the heights snap to whole rows and the title does not scale. Reduced\nmotion drops the picture's travel and its stretch; the cover, the title and the description still\nfollow the scroll, since they are the collapse itself."],[1305,"The frame fills its parent, clips what is in it, and holds its viewport and its buttons; the list's\ncontent is 32 points apart; a button floats 16 points from its edge."],[355,"The frame is `shape.container` (12 points) with 12 points of room either side and 8 above and below;\nthe turn's type is the body's size (13 points with a pointer, 15 on a touch screen) with a step of\nleading, in the ink the fill carries — `primary-foreground` on `default`, the page's text color on\nthe neutral fills, `destructive` on `destructive`. Reactions are 6 points either side, 3 above and\nbelow, 4 apart, ringed 3 points in the card's color."],[247,"The frame is as wide as what it holds (`default` 160 points wide at least, 120 when vertical), in\nthe card's fill with a hairline edge and the card's corners (`inset`'s at `xs`), 10 / 8 / 6 points\nin from its edge and 8 / 6 / 4 above and below; the title is medium in the page's ink and the\ndescription the note's size in the muted ink. The frame always pads as if it holds text, so a\nmedia on its own sits in that padding. `attachmentGlyph(size, orientation)` is exported for a glyph an app draws itself."],[321,"The framed surface of one turn of a conversation, and the reactions that hang off it."],[2064,"The glyphs are drawn while the pointer is over the lights, as the system draws them, and the\nlights turn grey while the window is in the background."],[823,"The grabber"],[1009,"The group draws the field: its fill, its edge and, while the input inside has the keys, the focus edge and its ring. The input inside draws none of that, so a group has one frame, not two."],[371,"The group is `role=\"group\"`, so a reader hears the buttons as one set."],[590,"The header is the top of the page it stands in: at `expandedHeight` while the page is at its top,\ndown to `collapsedHeight` once the page has scrolled `expandedHeight - collapsedHeight`. Everything\nfollows `scroll` — the scroll view's `scrollValue` — in interpolated styles, so the collapse runs on\nthe UI thread with no JS per frame. The picture travels at half the scroll and stretches when the\npage is pulled past its top, a cover in the header's fill fades over it, the title shrinks into the\nbar and the description fades out above it. The header sticks to the top of the scroll view it is\nthe first child of, so it holds there once collapsed."],[1378,"The header keeps its height and only its title changes, so the content starts under the bar at every\noffset — which is what the platform's large title does there too. The header is a function so the\npage closes its own offset over it, and the title's child is an accessor, so the title re-reads the\noffset as the page moves."],[1355,"The header on a desktop"],[1369,"The header's back button appears while there is a page below it, on the leading edge: this library's\nghost `IconButton` with a `ChevronLeft`, named \"Back\". It does what a platform's back does — calls\n`onBackPress`, then pops unless `popOnBack` is `false`:"],[1081,"The heading is the app's: a row of the letter and its count on `colors.card`, 34 points tall. To pull the list down to reload it, put a `RefreshIndicator` over it."],[176,"The icon comes first, drawn at `ALERT_ICON` (16 points) in the notice's ink — the terminal draws\nno icons, and there the notice is its text. The title and the note line up in the column beside\nit, the note a step quieter than the title."],[170,"The icon's column"],[494,"The indicator travels to `index`; each item shows the `CarouselDot` you give it. The carousel keeps\nthe slide shown to itself (`nsh/base` only reports it through `onChange`), so an item with no child\ndraws nothing: pass the dot, and tell it which slide it is on."],[988,"The ink is `on-media` and the ground is the theme's `media`, the pair that stays dark in either appearance and in every theme; the caption strip and the close chip carry a wash of that ink so they read over any photograph."],[362,"The join"],[1129,"The label does not press the control: nsh has no `for` to hang the pair on. A row that toggles its\ncontrol is a `listRow` of the app's, as the `Checkbox` page shows."],[2467,"The library's own: graphite surfaces, one citrus accent, 8-point controls and 14-point cards."],[1181,"The line is in the muted ink at the body's size (13 points with a pointer, 15 on a touch screen),\nits icon 16 points in the same ink; a marker's icon box is never shrunk by a tight row. In a\nterminal the icon is drawn as NSH draws icons there (a check is `✓`, the others show nothing) and\nthe rules are hairlines."],[1712,"The line is one point of the theme's edge color (`colors.border`, `theme.json`'s `border`), with\nno other look: a corner is the caller's `style`, and so is its room."],[2225,"The list is generated from `registry.json` and these pages, so every `registry:ui` item has a page and every page has an item. A page registered on an NSH element is Base. A page registered on none is Enhanced."],[397,"The look alone"],[1005,"The look alone"],[1525,"The look alone"],[1568,"The look alone"],[2042,"The look alone"],[2109,"The look alone"],[2132,"The look alone"],[1417,"The look is `navigationMenuLook()` — `root`, `list`, `item`, `panel`, `link`, `linkText`,\n`linkHint` — and `navigationMenuTriggerLook({ open })` for a trigger. Spread them on the part they\nname."],[2299,"The look is `theme.json` (the roles of both appearances, three `radius-*` layer lengths, the fixed `scrim` / `media` / `on-media` values and any font families) plus `lib/ui/theme.ts`, the readers; the deeper half of a theme — surfaces, feedback, controls, metrics — is the spec in `themes/<id>.ts`, which `add theme-<id>` installs beside the build-time theme. `init` installs the build-time theme and points each `nsh.json` at `theme.json`; in a monorepo they live under `packages/ui`. See Theme specification for the keys, and Customizing to derive a theme or to change a copied component."],[1533,"The look is a function, for a `nsh/base` control of your own. Its `shape.control` corners shape the focus ring; a `style` with its own radius moves the ring with it."],[414,"The look is a function, for a pressable of your own."],[2119,"The look is a function, for a pressable of your own."],[2399,"The names are `button`, `badge`, `field`, `choice`, `switch`, `slider`, `progress`, `spinner`, `popup`, `list`, `tabs`, `menubar` and `well`."],[179,"The note has a column and no row of its own: with no title it sits beside the icon,\nand a long sentence wraps there. Put an error that needs more than one line in the note, not in\nthe title, which keeps to one line."],[1746,"The open state"],[65,"The packages the items import (`lucide`) are installed with the app's own package manager, unless its `package.json` already declares them at a version in range: an exact `\"lucide\": \"1.66.0\"` is left as written."],[1150,"The page a link names is the one shown."],[1745,"The page beside it"],[1386,"The page's look — spread it on the views it names, or read its words for content of your own:\n`navPageLook()` (`page`, `header`, `title`, `backGlyph`)."],[2525,"The palettes the demos can draw in: what a theme is, the radius layers, and a page for each palette."],[1727,"The panel's look is `sheetPanel(side)`."],[908,"The parts are optional: a line on its own is centered in the room the block has."],[831,"The pill at the top edge is 100 by 8 points, fully round, in the muted fill, and it shows on a\nbottom drawer only, the one it can be dragged from. A sheet's grabber is the narrower pill. The\ngrabber is drawn by the component, not by the app."],[1981,"The pill track is as wide as its tabs, the tabs as wide as their labels; an underline is as wide as\nits labels and its rule. A touch screen always spans the row, and `fill` spans it with a pointer."],[1370,"The platform's own back works beside it: Escape, Alt+Left, the mouse's back button and the browser's\nback on the desktops, the swipe on iOS and predictive back on Android. `nsh/base` takes those, calls\n`onBackPress`, and removes the route from the stack's state, so `routes()` follows without the app\ndoing anything."],[883,"The popover goes in the addon, where the smile goes: `PopoverContent` is what mounts the popover,\nand it draws the trigger in its own place — a `PopoverContent` written outside the group leaves the\nsmile under the field. `placement=\"top\"` puts the panel over the field, where a composer at the\nbottom of a window has room; on a narrow frame give the panel a narrower `class` and the popover\nthe same `width`."],[1779,"The provider owns the state and lays the column and the page out; a column and its parts draw on\n`nsh`'s `View` (`SidebarSeparator` wraps this library's `Separator`, and the phone's sheet is this\nlibrary's `Sheet`). Every `nsh` view prop passes through; a row is an `nsh/base` button's props too."],[83,"The public registry is `registry.json` built into `public/r` and served from GitHub. To host your own, build the same files and pass its URL to `--registry`."],[1827,"The rail and the knob"],[1523,"The role it reads as"],[30,"The root `nsh.json` lists the exports: the component barrel, the theme runtime, each built-in theme, and every component. `public/r` is the registry the CLI copies from. Both are built from `packages/ui` and `packages/theme`."],[650,"The root owns the open flag and the chosen values; `ComboboxContent` mounts `nsh/base` Combobox and every base prop passes through except those. The combobox look, `listboxProps` and `OptionRow` fill the field, the popup and the rows by default."],[972,"The root owns the open flag and the two delays (`openDelay`, 600 ms by default; `closeDelay`, 300):\n`Infinity` in either makes the card wait for the app, which is how a card a press opens keeps still\nunder the pointer. `HoverCardContent` mounts\n`nsh/base` HoverCard and every base prop passes through\nexcept the open state, the delays and the lifecycle callbacks, which the root owns. Its children\ncome in on a `PopIn` with `popoverLook`."],[1689,"The root owns the open flag and the value; `SelectContent` mounts `nsh/base` Select and every base prop passes through except those. `listboxProps` and `OptionRow` fill the popup, the scrim and the rows by default."],[1507,"The root owns the open flag; `PopoverContent` mounts `nsh/base` Popover and every base prop passes through except those. Its children come in on a `PopIn` with `popoverLook` (the surface, 14 points of padding with a pointer and 16 on a touch screen); `class` and `style` go over that surface."],[1409,"The root owns the open item and the two delays; a panel mounts `nsh/base` Popover\nand every base prop passes through except the trigger, the open state and the lifecycle callbacks."],[1582,"The round chip a pull to refresh draws over a scroll view, traveling in with the pull and spinning while it refreshes."],[1776,"The row above it opens it. `nsh/base`'s `Collapsible` does the reveal — not this library's\n`Collapsible`, which draws a disclosure row of its own (a settings list wants that; a sidebar row\nwants the row it already has):"],[1347,"The row is its avatar and its content 8 points apart, the content a column 10 points apart in the\nbody's size; the header and the footer are small (12 with a pointer, 13 on a touch screen), medium\nand in the muted ink, 12 points in from the turn's edge."],[674,"The rows are made as they come into view, so five thousand files cost what five do: `listClass`\ngives the list its height and the rest scrolls. `keywords` let the query find a file by its\nextension as well as by its name (`png` finds every image), `renderItem` draws the extension's\nicon, and the ref's `matchedCount()` is what the query left — the count under the palette reads\nit, never the app's guess at the filter."],[1228,"The rows are the menu's"],[25,"The same shell serves pointer, touch, and terminal. Heights, icons, padding, and hit targets come from the theme's `pointer` and `touch` tiers. In the terminal, row and column sizes come from the theme's terminal metrics. Keep content inside the safe area with `env(safe-area-inset-*)` on padding and margin. Read `safeArea()` only when the layout has to do arithmetic with the inset."],[592,"The scene of the demo: a header over a list whose week headings stick under the bar. The bar's\nheight is `collapsingHeaderLook().heights(expandedHeight, collapsedHeight).bar` — the height the\nheader draws, whole rows in a terminal — so `stickyHeaderOffset` takes it rather than the number\npassed in."],[1590,"The scroll view (or `List`) keeps `onRefresh`, `refreshing`, `pullValue` and `refreshIndicator`: `refreshIndicator` hides the system's spinner, makes a pull refresh once it reaches `height`, and keeps `height` above the content while refreshing; `pullValue` is the animated value it feeds with how far it is pulled. `RefreshIndicator` only draws that value, over the top of the scroll view: its opacity, travel and scale follow `pull` on the UI thread, with no JS per frame. The arrow turns over once the pull is past half of `height`; while `refreshing` it gives way to a ring that spins. The strip takes no presses, so it sits above the scroll view without blocking it. Pass the same `height` to both."],[1273,"The scroller opens at the end, on the newest turn, and keeps itself there: when the content grows\n— a turn is sent, a stream answers — the list keeps the end in view. Scroll up and it stops\nfollowing; the view stays where the reader put it while new turns arrive below. Scroll back to the\nend and it follows again."],[1052,"The segments keep the field's own width — a bar of four parts, each a share of it — and the word\nkeeps a column at the end, so the meter does not reflow as the word changes from `Empty` to\n`Strong`."],[341,"The sender's bottom corner is squarer than the rest. It is a recipe, not a prop — a `style` on\n`BubbleContent` that reads the theme's `shape`. Use the logical corner (`borderBottomEndRadius` for\nwhat you send, `borderBottomStartRadius` for what came in) so it follows the reading direction."],[1708,"The separator is the line and nothing else: the room above and below it is the caller's."],[871,"The session types the props name are re-exported from this file, so an app does not import them from `nsh/base`: `EditorRef`, `EditorDecoration`, `EditorDiagnostic`, `EditorPosition`, `EditorRange`, `EditorSelection`, `EditorSearchResult`, `EditorCommand`, `EditorCompletionItem`, and the rest of the editor's value types."],[1806,"The shape of content that is still coming."],[2433,"The shared values"],[2452,"The shared values"],[2471,"The shared values"],[2484,"The shared values"],[2497,"The shared values"],[2510,"The shared values"],[2259,"The shell is the component and its parts, above the look. It reads props, spreads the look, and gives a composite its roles."],[2419,"The side-effect import is deliberate: TypeScript drops an import whose value is unused, and the font with it, when the theme names the family as a string. A weight or an italic is a file of its own, `registerFont(bytes)` reads one the app did not ship, and the fallback after the app's family is what NSH draws for a family it cannot find — see NSH's fonts."],[1861,"The sidebar button"],[22,"The sketch is the shape of the file, not a copy of `button.tsx`. `buttonLook` is a real shared export. Keyboard, focus, and dismissal stay in `nsh/base`."],[1354,"The stack and its routes"],[302,"The steps are a row that wraps when the window is narrow; the page the trail ends at is\n`BreadcrumbPage`, in the page's text color, and is announced as the current page."],[192,"The surface is the card's, with one hairline edge and the theme's inset corners, padded 16 by 12;\nthe icon's column is as wide as the icon, the text's takes the rest, a gap of 12 between them.\n`ALERT_ICON` is the size its icon is drawn at."],[1587,"The system spinner"],[1192,"The system's menu"],[716,"The table fills and scrolls in the box it is given."],[1103,"The terminal"],[2336,"The terminal draws no icons: `Icon` shows nothing there, except `Check`, which is the mark `✓`. An icon-only control uses `IconLabel` instead, which shows a short word in the icon's place:"],[2000,"The terminal starts nothing: `term.write(bytes)` shows what the program printed, `onData` carries what the user typed or pasted (keys the program expects, the emulator's answers included), and `onResize` reports the size in cells. `term.reset()` clears the screen and the scrollback, `term.text()` gives every line, `term.focus()` takes the keyboard."],[2413,"The theme file stays complete: copy it, then edit. Every role of both appearances is there, and a colour the app adds must be named in both (a token in one alone is not a token):"],[2351,"The theme is the upstream gallery's: a graphite page (paper in light appearance), cards and wells, one citrus accent, compact controls with a pointer and roomier ones on a touch screen. It is **one TypeScript module** — the spec `defineTheme()` compiles — plus the module that reads it, and every component takes its colors, radii, typography, motion and depth from there. `theme.json` and `DESIGN.md` are exports of that same spec, so a theme is written once and drawn, shipped and documented from one place. No component writes a color of its own."],[2066,"The three parts are the shapes the platforms draw; an app that wants its own draws the same\n`desktop` views itself, and the platform still carries out the press. A round close button\n(`Icon` from `lib/ui/icons`, `X` from `lucide`; the traffic lights keep macOS's own hues\nwhatever the theme, so the disc's colour is written out rather than read from a role, and the\nglyph on a light is black at half):"],[730,"The time of day"],[1932,"The title is beside the switch, not in it, so the switch carries the row's name in `accessibilityLabel`; it is still read as that switch and its state. A list of them is a `FieldGroup`, and `FieldSeparator` draws the hairline between two rows."],[1663,"The track is the theme's `channel` and the thumb its `plate`, both read where they are drawn: a\nwell holding a raised block. The thumb is always as big as a segment's own box, so the pointer's\nhover shade covers exactly the box the chosen block covers. Every theme draws this one form — the\nlist reads no control dimension, so `apple`'s capsule-in-a-capsule and `material`'s connected\ncells are gone; what the native controls look like instead is in the theme's difference table\n(`packages/theme/themes/material/README.md` / `packages/theme/themes/apple/README.md`)."],[1938,"The track, border (`switch.edge.off`, `switch.edge.on`, `switch.edge.color`), knob dimensions (`switch.track`, `switch.knob.*`), and derived press feedback all read `control(\"switch\")` (`@shadcn-nsh/theme`) so switching themes updates geometry and motion on the next frame:"],[290,"The trail from where the app starts to the page it shows."],[307,"The trail is a `navigation` landmark named `\"Breadcrumb\"`; the rows under it carry no role. `nsh`\nhas no `listitem` role, so `BreadcrumbList` is not a `list` either: a list whose children are not\nits items reads worse than a plain row, and `ItemGroup` makes the same call."],[319,"The trail is small type in the muted ink, 6 points between steps (10 on a touch screen); a step's\nlink takes the text color while the pointer rests on it and the theme's focus ring with the keyboard\non it. `BreadcrumbPage` sets `accessibilityState.current` to `\"page\"`; pass your own\n`accessibilityState` to say something else. The separator is hidden from screen readers. In a\nterminal, which draws no icons, the separator is `›` and the ellipsis `…`. `breadcrumbLook()` is\nexported for a trail an app draws itself."],[1282,"The trigger fires on the frame's start side — the top of a normal list — so the same call also\nworks over an inverted list, where older turns sit at the top."],[1233,"The trigger of the menu that is open wears the neutral `accent` fill — the modal-less bar says which\nmenu it belongs to without the bar changing — and the pointer over another trigger whispers the same\nfill. A press opens one menu and a press closes it; once one is open, the pointer moves between\nmenus. A disabled trigger takes no presses and dims to 50%; a string child is the trigger's name, in\nthe menu's type; anything else (an icon and a name) is drawn as given."],[643,"The trigger opens and closes the list. The field's input takes typing, and typing opens the list\nfiltered as you type (`nsh/base` also toggles on the input's press — `references/nsh/crates/nsh-base/src/combobox.rs`,\nits `on_press` — but a click on macOS lands in the field editor for the caret,\n`references/nsh/crates/nsh-platform-macos/src/text_input.rs`, so with a pointer the arrow is the way\nin). Clicking the field puts the caret there and does not open the list; tabbing into it does not\neither."],[107,"The trigger takes more than one child: what you draw is laid out in the row before the chevron, in\none line, and the chevron stays at the end (it is the part that turns as the item opens)."],[1226,"The trigger that is open"],[1684,"The trigger's edge takes the danger tone."],[287,"The view props (`style`, `class`, `testID`, `role`, the accessibility ones) are spread on the capsule after the badge's own, so an app can name it to a screen reader or take it out of the layout without a wrapper."],[982,"The viewer is open while `image` is set; `onClose` is where it asks to close (the close button, Escape, the back button, a press on the ground, a swipe down), so set `image` back to `null` there. A pinch zooms to 4×, a double tap or click to 2.5× at the point, a drag pans the zoomed image, and at 1× a drag down closes it. The `alt` text is the caption and the image's name for screen readers."],[1271,"The viewport is `nsh`'s `List`: `each` is the conversation in reading\norder, oldest first, and the child renders one row. It is called once per cell, not once per turn —\nread `item()` where the turn is used (`item().text`), because the cell is reused for another turn as\nthe reader scrolls."],[1117,"The visible name of a control, beside it or over a group of them."],[2273,"Theme"],[787,"Theme colors"],[2427,"Theme rules: `docs/theme.md` and `packages/theme/README.md`. Behavior and accessibility are `nsh/base`'s: nsh.dev/elements."],[2338,"Theme specification"],[2524,"Themes"],[34,"Themes is each built-in theme."],[987,"There are no `ImageViewerClose` or `ImageViewerCaption` parts. The close button is always there and the caption is always the `alt`, so a part would only be a place to put what the viewer already draws. A gallery of several images in one viewer is the app's: set `image` to the next one."],[1536,"There is no axis: a pressable has one look — the hover, the sink and the ring — and its appearance\nis its content's. `pressableLook` takes `disabled` (no hover or press feedback), `scale` (how far a\npress sinks it) and `style` (geometry over the look's), and is what the reference page spreads on\n`nsh/base`'s Button."],[1820,"There is no axis: a skeleton is one look — the theme's `accent` fill, the theme's control corners,\nthe breathing. The box, including a radius of the app's own, is the caller's `style`."],[1137,"There is no other axis: the text is `controlSize.label` (14 points with a pointer, 15 on a touch\nscreen) and medium, in the theme's text color, a row with a gap of 8. `disabled` dims that row to\nhalf."],[1544,"Thin rail"],[1131,"This `Label` is the visible name, and nothing more: it names nothing for a screen reader. nsh has\nno `htmlFor` and no `labelledBy`, so the control's own `accessibilityLabel` is its name — the same\nwords, said twice:"],[2314,"This copies `lib/ui/icons.tsx` and does not install an npm package. A component that draws icons of its own (`select`, `menu`, `tabs`, `sidebar`…) brings `icons` when it is added. With the TSX `nshm` install the same module is `shadcn-nsh`'s `Icon` export; nothing else is installed."],[2420,"This library distributes no font files: `add theme` writes JSON and TypeScript and names no family."],[8,"This page is the map of the library you install. Behavior stays in `nsh/base`. Appearance comes from the theme. The file you import, or copy, is a styled shell. The same source is published two ways, and neither way adds a third-party runtime dependency."],[84,"This repository has both steps as scripts: `bun run registry:build` and `bun run registry:serve`."],[2442,"This theme maps Apple's system colors and Liquid Glass onto the shared control slots. It does not clone every UIKit or AppKit widget. The official reference is the Apple Human Interface Guidelines."],[2519,"This theme maps Fluent 2 and WinUI 3 onto the shared control slots: Accent blue, 4- and 8-point circular corners, in-app Acrylic, and decelerating motion. It does not clone every WinUI control. The official reference is Windows App Design."],[2461,"This theme maps Material 3 Expressive onto the shared control slots: tonal surfaces, circular corners, and state layers. It does not clone every Material widget. The official reference is Material Design 3."],[1758,"Three axes say how it meets the window:"],[1860,"Three columns"],[2371,"Three more values do not turn with the appearance and are named in `cssVars.theme` beside the radii: `scrim` (what a modal dims the page with), `media` (the ground a photo or a video sits on) and `on-media` (ink drawn straight onto one)."],[182,"Three things are called an alert here, and this is the quiet one:"],[1567,"Tile"],[2037,"Time Field"],[2049,"TitleBar"],[2425,"To change one component's look instead, edit its look function in the copied component; to change structure or API, edit the shell. See Customizing."],[2075,"Toast"],[2094,"Toggle"],[2431,"Tokens"],[2450,"Tokens"],[2469,"Tokens"],[2482,"Tokens"],[2495,"Tokens"],[2508,"Tokens"],[277,"Tone"],[783,"Tone"],[1437,"Tone"],[1543,"Tone"],[1889,"Tone"],[1909,"Tone"],[2124,"Toolbar"],[2149,"Tooltip"],[2173,"Trace"],[2055,"Traffic lights"],[2199,"Tree"],[129,"Trigger"],[1890,"Truncate"],[2266,"TSX `nshm`"],[1267,"Turns"],[2237,"Two kinds of change. A theme reaches every component. A copied file reaches that component. The TSX `nshm` install does not copy files, so appearance changes there go through Custom themes. Editing a shell or a look is the copy-into-app CLI, in Customizing copied components."],[732,"Two months"],[1855,"Two or three columns of navigatio